window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Messages and Encodings", "chapter": "Information and Distinguishability", "url": "01-information-and-distinguishability.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 Messages and Encodings Alice sends Bob a physical object to mean either “yes” or “no.” If the two possible preparations always look identical to every permitted observation, no message has been conveyed. Information begins with distinguishable alternatives, a preparation rule, and a receiver’s procedure. 1.1.1 Physical carriers and distinguishable alternatives A carrier might be a light pulse, a spin, or a mark on paper. The logical message is not the material itself: an encoding associates each message with a physical preparation. A decoder associates an observation with a guess. Two preparations are perfectly distinguishable for a task if an allowed observation identifies which occurred with certainty. Imperfect detectors and overlapping physical responses turn that certainty into a probability. 1.1.2 States and knowledge about states A state description collects predictions for possible observations. It is distinct from a name assigned to the preparation and from the receiver’s knowledge of that name. If Alice chooses between two preparations secretly, Bob may need an uncertain description even though Alice knows her choice. Later, quantum theory will show that a pure state itself can have unpredictable measurement outcomes; those probabilities need not come from ignorance of a classical label. Problem 1.1. With the detector above, what is the posterior probability of A after a non-red report? Worked solution The weights are (3/4)(4/5)=3/5 for A and (1/4)(1/5)=1/20 for B. The total is 13/20, so the posterior for A is 12/13."}, {"title": "Uncertainty", "chapter": "Information and Distinguishability", "url": "01-information-and-distinguishability.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Uncertainty 1.2.1 Possible messages and prior knowledge Suppose Alice sends A on three quarters of trials and B on one quarter. These frequencies, or a model assigning those probabilities, are Bob’s prior information. Without observing anything, always guessing A succeeds with probability 3/4. A proposed detector must be compared with that baseline, not with a fair guess. Probabilities are nonnegative numbers summing to one over mutually exclusive exhaustive possibilities. 1.2.2 Observations and inference Let a detector report red with probability 1/5 for A and 4/5 for B. The joint probabilities of red with A and red with B are (3/4)(1/5)=3/20 and (1/4)(4/5)=1/5. Red occurs with total probability 7/20. Among red events, the fraction from B is (1/5)/(7/20)=4/7. This division is conditioning: update the alternatives using the observation and normalize their weights. A red report favors B even though A was more likely before observation. Problem 1.2. How well does the rule “red means B, otherwise A” perform? Worked solution Correct events have probability (1/4)(4/5)+(3/4)(4/5)=4/5. This improves on the prior-only success 3/4."}, {"title": "Information Tasks", "chapter": "Information and Distinguishability", "url": "01-information-and-distinguishability.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 Information Tasks 1.3.1 Learning, preserving, and transmitting Learning a message, preserving an unknown state, and transmitting a carrier are different tasks. A procedure that identifies a preparation may disturb the carrier. A procedure that faithfully transfers it may leave the receiver unable to identify it from a single observation. We will keep the physical transformation and the classical information extracted from it separate. 1.3.2 Resources and criteria for success State the allowed preparations, prior probabilities, observations, output, and success criterion before comparing protocols. Resources include uses of a channel, transmitted systems, prior shared correlations, and classical messages. More complicated physical descriptions do not automatically carry more accessible information. The receiver must have a procedure that actually extracts what the task requests. Problem 1.3. Does a carrier with many possible physical settings guarantee many readable messages? Worked solution No. Their responses may overlap, the receiver may have limited measurements, or the preparation and noise may prevent reliable discrimination. We next quantify classical uncertainty and the resources needed to compress or protect messages."}, {"title": "Probability and Correlation", "chapter": "Classical Information", "url": "02-classical-information.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 Probability and Correlation A source sends many zeros and a few ones. Can a long message use fewer bits without losing its meaning? The answer depends on the distribution of messages, the allowed errors, and how long a block we encode. 2.1.1 Joint and conditional probabilities For two random variables X,Y, \\(p(x,y)\\) is the probability of a joint event. Summing over y gives \\(p(x)\\). When p(x)>0, define \\(p(y|x)=p(x,y)/p(x)\\); equivalently joint probability is prior times conditional probability. A binary flip channel with error e has \\(p(y=x|x)=1-e\\) and \\(p(y\\ne x|x)=e\\). These equations describe a distribution, not an individual deterministic event. 2.1.2 Independent and correlated messages Independence means p(x,y)=p(x)p(y). A pair that is 00 or 11 with equal probability has fair individual bits but correlated joint values; observing one removes uncertainty about the other. Independent source uses are a substantive assumption. Repeated signals can have memory, and coding theorems for independent repetitions cannot be transferred to them without analysis. Problem 2.1. What is the entropy of a certain message? Worked solution Zero: its only nonzero probability is one and −log₂1=0. No choice remains to communicate if the receiver already knows the message."}, {"title": "Entropy and Compression", "chapter": "Classical Information", "url": "02-classical-information.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 Entropy and Compression 2.2.1 Information content and average length Among N equally likely messages, identifying one requires log₂N binary choices in the ideal counting sense. The logarithm is the inverse of exponentiation: log₂8=3. For a particular event of probability p, the information content −log₂p is additive for independent events because their probabilities multiply. Averaging defines \\(H(X)=-\\sum_xp(x)\\log_2p(x)\\), with a zero-probability term assigned zero by continuity. For probabilities 1/2,1/4,1/8,1/8, the prefix code 0,10,110,111 has average length 7/4 bits, equal to H. No codeword prefixes another, so concatenated messages can be decoded. 2.2.2 Repeated sources and compression limits For an independent binary source with one-probability 1/4, the entropy is h₂(1/4)≈0.811 bits per signal. A long typical string contains about a quarter ones. The number of high-probability strings grows roughly as 2^(nH), so labels of about nH bits can describe them while atypical strings get a separate treatment. Shannon’s noiseless coding theorem makes this asymptotic statement precise. It does not say that one biased bit can always be stored losslessly in 0.811 of a physical bit. Problem 2.2. Can 0,01,1 be used as a prefix code? Worked solution No. The first word prefixes the second. A concatenation such as 01 can be ambiguous without extra framing."}, {"title": "Noisy Communication", "chapter": "Classical Information", "url": "02-classical-information.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 Noisy Communication 2.3.1 Channels and decoding Sending 0 as 000 and 1 as 111 lets majority decoding correct one flipped bit. For independent flip probability e, failure means two or three flips, with probability \\(3e^2(1-e)+e^3=3e^2-2e^3\\). At e=0.1 this is 0.028, below 0.1, but three channel uses now transmit one information bit. Redundancy trades rate for reliability. 2.3.2 Reliable transmission and rate A communication rate is information bits divided by channel uses. Reliability means the block error tends to zero along a sequence of longer codes at that rate. For the independent binary symmetric channel, capacity is 1−h₂(e) bits per use. At e=1/2 it is zero because the output is independent of the input. The general coding theorem and converse require a coding argument beyond the repetition example; the example illustrates error reduction but does not attain every rate below capacity. Problem 2.3. At e=0, what rate does the three-bit repetition code achieve? Worked solution Only 1/3 bit per channel use, despite a channel capacity of one. Being reliable does not make a code rate-optimal. Classical probabilities add across mutually exclusive alternatives. Interference requires a different kind of quantity before probabilities are formed. Sources and further study: Shannon, A Mathematical Theory of Communication ."}, {"title": "Amplitudes", "chapter": "Quantum States", "url": "03-quantum-states.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 Amplitudes A two-path experiment can yield a certain output when both paths are available, yet a fair output when a path record is made. Ordinary probabilities attached independently to the paths cannot describe that change. We introduce amplitudes as quantities that combine before the probability is calculated. 3.1.1 Interference and the probability rule Assign amplitudes a,b to two exclusive configuration labels 0,1. Their measured probabilities are \\(|a|^2,|b|^2\\), summing to one. This probability rule is a physical postulate supported by experiment; it is not derived merely from a desire for reversible algebra. The transformation H sends amplitudes (a,b) to \\(((a+b)/\\sqrt2,(a-b)/\\sqrt2)\\). Starting with (1,0), two H operations restore (1,0): the unwanted paths cancel in the second output. 3.1.2 Complex numbers and relative phase A relative minus sign changes H’s outcome, even though it leaves the original configuration probabilities unchanged. Continuous phase changes are represented by complex numbers. Write i²=−1 and \\(e^{i\\phi}=\\cos\\phi+i\\sin\\phi\\); its magnitude is one. For \\((1,e^{i\\phi})/\\sqrt2\\), an H readout gives zero-probability \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Multiplying both amplitudes by the same phase changes no prediction. Relative phase is physical in this experiment; global phase is not. Problem 3.1. Predict H applied to minus. Worked solution Minus has amplitudes (1,−1)/√2. The output amplitudes are (0,1), so the result is one with certainty."}, {"title": "State Representations", "chapter": "Quantum States", "url": "03-quantum-states.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 State Representations 3.2.1 Vectors, kets, and inner products A column of amplitudes is a vector. We write \\(\\ket\\psi=a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\), where kets name vectors and the coefficients specify their combination. The inner product \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi=c^*a+d^*b\\) for \\(\\ket\\phi=c\\ket0+d\\ket1\\) compares vectors; the star conjugates a complex number. Its squared magnitude is the probability of the projection test onto normalized φ. Orthogonal means inner product zero. 3.2.2 Normalization and distinguishability Normalization is \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=1\\). The states zero and one are orthogonal; zero and plus have overlap 1/√2. A zero-basis measurement on plus is fair, while the plus/minus basis distinguishes those two states perfectly. A pure vector is a preparation description, not a list of hidden outcomes for every possible measurement. Different measurement bases ask different physical questions. Problem 3.2. For (|0〉+i|1〉)/√2, what is the H readout probability of zero? Worked solution It is |(1+i)/2|²=1/2. The phase π/2 gives a fair readout, unlike phase zero or π."}, {"title": "Evolution and Readout", "chapter": "Quantum States", "url": "03-quantum-states.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 Evolution and Readout 3.3.1 Unitary transitions A linear transition U maps every normalized input to a normalized output exactly when \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\); the dagger conjugates and transposes its matrix. In a finite equal-dimensional space, this is a unitary operation with inverse U†. It preserves all inner products. H is its own inverse. This describes isolated reversible evolution; measurements and discarded systems will require a broader description. 3.3.2 Projective measurements and conditional states For orthogonal projectors Pj summing to I, outcome j has probability \\(\\|P_j\\psi\\|^2\\), and the conditional state is \\(P_j\\ket\\psi/\\sqrt{p_j}\\) when p_j>0. A rank-one test keeps one direction; a higher-rank test keeps a subspace and may preserve superposition within it. We draw a circuit wire as the evolving system, a labeled gate as a unitary, and M as measurement; time runs left to right. A double line represents its classical record. Problem 3.3. Can a unitary map both zero and one to plus? Worked solution No. Their original inner product is zero, while identical output vectors have inner product one. Unitary evolution preserves this distinction. The next chapter pairs configuration labels to describe two systems and discovers joint states that cannot be split into separate vectors. Relative phase becomes a measurable probability: the zero outcome occurs with probability (1+cos φ)/2."}, {"title": "Joint States", "chapter": "Composite Systems", "url": "04-composite-systems.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 Joint States Alice and Bob each hold a two-state system. Four configuration labels are needed for the pair: 00,01,10,11, with Alice’s bit first. Joint amplitudes contain more possibilities than independently assigning each party a state. 4.1.1 Product configurations and amplitudes If Alice has a|0〉+b|1〉 and Bob has c|0〉+d|1〉, their independently prepared joint amplitudes are ac,ad,bc,bd. This multiplication rule defines the tensor product, written \\(\\ket\\psi_A\\otimes\\ket\\phi_B\\). A general joint state instead has four coefficients \\(\\alpha_{00},\\alpha_{01},\\alpha_{10},\\alpha_{11}\\) with squared magnitudes summing to one. Tensor notation abbreviates the paired configurations; it does not add a new physical system. 4.1.2 Tensor products and local operations An operation acting only on Alice transforms her label in every term and leaves Bob’s label untouched. We write it U⊗I. For example H⊗I maps 00 to (00+10)/√2. CNOT is a joint operation: it maps |a,b〉 to |a,b XOR a〉. A circuit with H on Alice followed by CNOT from Alice to Bob prepares a Bell pair from 00. Problem 4.1. Is (|00〉+|01〉)/√2 entangled? Worked solution No. It factors as |0〉⊗|+〉. Two nonzero joint amplitudes are not enough to establish entanglement."}, {"title": "Entanglement", "chapter": "Composite Systems", "url": "04-composite-systems.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 Entanglement 4.2.1 Bell-state construction The resulting state is \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). It has equal-amplitude equal-bit configurations and zero unequal-bit amplitudes. This is a coherent superposition: applying H to both systems leaves the same Bell state. Consequently both computational-basis and plus/minus-basis measurements give equal results. 4.2.2 Failure of product descriptions Could it be a product? The coefficients would require ac=bd=1/√2 but ad=bc=0. Nonzero ac and bd force all four factors nonzero, contradicting the latter equations. More generally a two-qubit pure state is a product exactly when \\(\\alpha_{00}\\alpha_{11}-\\alpha_{01}\\alpha_{10}=0\\). Entanglement means a pure joint state fails this product description; mixed-state entanglement will require a broader definition. Problem 4.2. What does Z on Alice do to the Bell pair? Worked solution It changes the sign of the 11 amplitude, producing (00−11)/√2. The computational-basis counts stay the same, but plus/minus correlations change. Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "Joint Measurement", "chapter": "Composite Systems", "url": "04-composite-systems.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 Joint Measurement 4.3.1 Marginal and conditional probabilities For the Bell pair, each local outcome is fair. Summing joint probabilities over Bob’s bit gives Alice’s marginal probability one half for either bit. Conditional on Alice observing zero, Bob’s computational-basis result is zero with certainty. Conditional predictions differ from unconditioned remote predictions; Bob needs Alice’s record to sort his trials into those groups. 4.3.2 Correlations and classical comparison A classical mixture of 00 and 11 also gives perfectly correlated computational-basis results. In the plus/minus basis it gives all four pairs equally often, unlike the Bell pair. Thus correlations in one basis do not establish entanglement. The different joint phases and coherent amplitudes matter. We next need a common notation for the mixture and for a subsystem of the entangled state. Problem 4.3. Can Bob tell from his local counts whether Alice measured her Bell half? Worked solution Not without a record selecting her outcome. His unconditioned local distribution remains fair. A general no-signalling argument follows in Chapter 9. Density operators will combine uncertain preparations and reduced subsystem descriptions without confusing them with pure superpositions."}, {"title": "Uncertain Preparations", "chapter": "Mixed States", "url": "05-mixed-states.html#section-5-1", "text": "5.1 Uncertain Preparations A fair coin chooses whether to prepare zero or one. A different apparatus supplies one half of a Bell pair. These procedures have different joint histories, yet every local measurement has the same statistics. We need a description based on those predictions. 5.1.1 Pure states and ensembles A mixture selects \\(\\ket{\\psi_j}\\) with probability p_j, so a projection onto φ has probability \\(\\sum_jp_j|\\braket\\phi{\\psi_j}|^2\\). This is an average of probabilities, not the squared magnitude of a sum of amplitudes. In particular a fair mixture of zero and one differs from plus: the mixture gives a fair X readout, while plus gives a certain one. 5.1.2 Constructing density operators Define \\(\\rho=\\sum_jp_j\\ket{\\psi_j}\\bra{\\psi_j}\\). The outer product is a matrix; it keeps exactly the coefficients needed for the averaged prediction \\(\\bra\\phi\\rho\\ket\\phi\\). For a general measurement operator E, the probability is tr(Eρ), where trace means the sum of diagonal entries. A density operator is positive, meaning \\(\\bra v\\rho\\ket v\\geq0\\), and has trace one. The fair basis mixture is diag(1/2,1/2)=I/2. Problem 5.1. Compute the density matrix of plus. Worked solution It is \\(\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}\\). The off-diagonal entries distinguish it from I/2."}, {"title": "Subsystems", "chapter": "Mixed States", "url": "05-mixed-states.html#section-5-2", "text": "5.2 Subsystems 5.2.1 Reduced states and partial traces For a joint state, sum over the unobserved subsystem index: \\((\\rho_A)_{a,a^{\\prime}}=\\sum_b(\\rho_{AB})_{ab,a^{\\prime}b}\\). This partial trace is designed so every Alice-only measurement has the same prediction from ρA as from the full state. Applying it to Φ+ removes the off-diagonal joint terms because their Bob labels differ, leaving I/2. 5.2.2 Entanglement and local uncertainty The Bell pair is globally pure but locally mixed. Local uncertainty therefore need not mean that a hidden local pure state was selected from a preferred ensemble. Alice’s reduced matrix is enough for Alice-only predictions, but it cannot predict correlations with Bob without the joint matrix. The global description carries information not contained in the separate reduced states. Problem 5.2. Why does tracing out Bob kill |00〉〈11|? Worked solution The partial trace sums terms with the same Bob index on the ket and bra. Here those indices are zero and one, whose overlap is zero. Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "Equivalent Descriptions", "chapter": "Mixed States", "url": "05-mixed-states.html#section-5-3", "text": "5.3 Equivalent Descriptions 5.3.1 Different ensembles for the same state The same matrix has many decompositions: \\(I/2=(\\ket0\\bra0+\\ket1\\bra1)/2=(\\ket+\\bra++\\ket-\\bra-)/2\\). Expand the latter sum: off-diagonal entries cancel. No measurement on the system alone can reveal which of these ensemble procedures was used. An external preparation record can distinguish them, but that is extra accessible information. 5.3.2 Purification and inaccessible records An ensemble can be represented by a pure joint state \\(\\ket\\Psi=\\sum_j\\sqrt{p_j}\\ket{\\psi_j}\\ket j_E\\), where the environment labels j are orthogonal. Discarding E gives ρ because cross terms have zero environment overlap. This is a purification. It is a mathematical representation of the same local predictions, not a claim that the observer can control the purifying system. For diagonal probabilities 3/4 and 1/4, a purification is \\(\\sqrt3\\ket{00}/2+\\ket{11}/2\\). Problem 5.3. Can the density matrix identify a unique preparation ensemble? Worked solution No. Different ensembles can give identical matrices and hence identical predictions for every measurement on the system alone. Interaction with an auxiliary system followed by discarding produces the general operations studied next."}, {"title": "Physical Constructions", "chapter": "Quantum Operations", "url": "06-quantum-operations.html#section-6-1", "text": "6.1 Physical Constructions Let a qubit control an initially zero auxiliary via CNOT, then ignore the auxiliary. The joint evolution is reversible, yet the qubit loses its visible interference. Irreversibility can arise from limiting which systems we retain. 6.1.1 Ancillas, interaction, and discarding For \\(\\ket\\psi=a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\), the interaction produces \\(a\\ket{00}+b\\ket{11}\\). Tracing the auxiliary out gives diag(|a|²,|b|²). The off-diagonal terms have vanished locally because the auxiliary keeps a distinguishing record. If we retain both systems and reverse CNOT, the initial pure qubit returns. Losing access to the auxiliary changes the available recovery. 6.1.2 Reversible and irreversible descriptions The construction is: initialize an auxiliary state, apply a joint unitary, and discard some output systems. Resetting a qubit is an extreme example: swap it with a zero auxiliary and discard the old state. This is a valid operation on the retained system, but not an invertible unitary there. The information went into another system rather than being reversibly merged into one state. Problem 6.1. At p=1/2, what does phase-flip noise do to plus? Worked solution Its off-diagonal entries vanish, producing I/2. At p=1 it instead produces the pure minus state; a known deterministic phase flip is reversible. Measure the auxiliary after CNOT to record the system’s Z value. Ignoring the record gives dephasing; conditioning on it gives a projective state update."}, {"title": "Quantum Channels", "chapter": "Quantum Operations", "url": "06-quantum-operations.html#section-6-2", "text": "6.2 Quantum Channels 6.2.1 Operator-sum representations For a pure zero auxiliary, define \\(K_j=\\bra j_EU\\ket0_E\\). Discarding the auxiliary gives the operator-sum rule \\(\\mathcal E(\\rho)=\\sum_jK_j\\rho K_j^\\dagger\\). For complete dephasing, K0=|0〉〈0| and K1=|1〉〈1|. These operators describe an unobserved environmental branch; they are not necessarily outcomes available to the user. 6.2.2 Trace preservation and complete positivity Trace preservation requires \\(\\sum_jK_j^\\dagger K_j=I\\). Positivity must remain true even when the input is entangled with an untouched reference; this is complete positivity. The auxiliary-unitary construction guarantees it. Ordinary matrix transposition preserves positivity of a single matrix but fails this stronger requirement: partial transposition of a Bell density matrix has an eigenvalue −1/2. It cannot be a physical channel on one half of every entangled input. Problem 6.2. Verify trace preservation for amplitude damping. Worked solution \\(K_0^\\dagger K_0=\\mathrm{diag}(1,1-\\gamma)\\) and \\(K_1^\\dagger K_1=\\mathrm{diag}(0,\\gamma)\\), whose sum is I."}, {"title": "Noise and Composition", "chapter": "Quantum Operations", "url": "06-quantum-operations.html#section-6-3", "text": "6.3 Noise and Composition 6.3.1 Simple qubit channels A phase-flip channel is \\((1-p)\\rho+pZ\\rho Z\\); its off-diagonal entries are multiplied by 1−2p. An amplitude-damping channel has \\(K_0=\\ket0\\bra0+\\sqrt{1-\\gamma}\\ket1\\bra1\\), \\(K_1=\\sqrt\\gamma\\ket0\\bra1\\). On \\(\\rho=\\begin{pmatrix}a&c\\\\c^*&b\\end{pmatrix}\\), its output is \\(\\begin{pmatrix}a+\\gamma b&\\sqrt{1-\\gamma}c\\\\\\sqrt{1-\\gamma}c^*&(1-\\gamma)b\\end{pmatrix}\\). This explicitly moves excited population to zero while reducing coherence. 6.3.2 Channel composition and accessible information Composing channels multiplies their branch operators: if the first has Kj and the second Ll, the combined operators are LlKj. A receiver cannot gain optimal distinguishability by applying the same channel to both candidate inputs: any later decision procedure could also be performed after that channel as part of an original-input measurement. This operational data-processing argument concerns optimized accessible decisions, not every arbitrarily chosen measurement statistic. Problem 6.3. Does reversing the retained qubit alone undo complete dephasing? Worked solution No universal qubit-only inverse exists. Plus and minus both become I/2, so their distinction has been lost from the retained subsystem. A measurement is an operation whose branch record is retained. We now describe both the outcome probabilities and the conditional state changes."}, {"title": "Measurement Procedures", "chapter": "Quantum Measurement", "url": "07-quantum-measurement.html#section-7-1", "text": "7.1 Measurement Procedures A detector does two jobs: it produces an outcome and changes the system. Identical outcome frequencies can conceal different disturbances. We will describe those two jobs separately. 7.1.1 Coupling to an auxiliary system Couple a system to a zero auxiliary with U and measure the auxiliary in basis j. The branch operator on the system is \\(M_j=\\bra jU\\ket0\\). For input ρ, the outcome probability is \\(p_j=\\mathrm{tr}(M_j\\rho M_j^\\dagger)\\), and the conditional state is \\(M_j\\rho M_j^\\dagger/p_j\\). This construction is the channel picture of Chapter 6 with the environmental record now retained. 7.1.2 Outcomes and conditional operations For a CNOT with the system as control and the zero auxiliary as target, the branch operators are \\(M_0=\\ket0\\bra0\\) and \\(M_1=\\ket1\\bra1\\). Measuring the auxiliary therefore measures the system’s computational-basis value. If the input is plus, each result has probability one half and leaves the corresponding basis state. The circuit explains the record and the loss of visible interference together. Problem 7.1. For the noisy effect E0 above, what is p0 on plus? Worked solution It is the average of the two diagonal entries, 1/2. The value alone does not reveal the conditional state. Measure the auxiliary after CNOT to record the system’s Z value. Ignoring the record gives dephasing; conditioning on it gives a projective state update."}, {"title": "General Measurements", "chapter": "Quantum Measurement", "url": "07-quantum-measurement.html#section-7-2", "text": "7.2 General Measurements 7.2.1 Measurement effects and probabilities Define the effect \\(E_j=M_j^\\dagger M_j\\). Then \\(p_j=\\mathrm{tr}(E_j\\rho)\\), every effect is positive, and \\(\\sum_jE_j=I\\). A collection with these properties is a positive-operator-valued measure, or POVM. Effects need not be orthogonal projectors and need not have eigenvalues only zero and one. They describe outcome statistics, not the complete physical aftermath. 7.2.2 POVMs and physical implementations For example \\(E_0=\\mathrm{diag}(3/4,1/4)\\) and \\(E_1=I-E_0\\) give a noisy indication of the Z value. One implementation has \\(M_j=\\sqrt{E_j}\\), where the square root takes square roots of the nonnegative diagonal entries. More generally the map \\(\\ket\\psi\\mapsto\\sum_j\\ket j M_j\\ket\\psi\\) preserves inner products when the completeness equation holds; extending that map to a larger unitary realizes the measurement with an auxiliary. This establishes physical realizability without requiring the effects themselves to be projectors. Problem 7.2. Verify the square-root implementation’s completeness. Worked solution M0†M0+M1†M1=E0+E1=I, so total outcome probability is one for every input."}, {"title": "Information and Disturbance", "chapter": "Quantum Measurement", "url": "07-quantum-measurement.html#section-7-3", "text": "7.3 Information and Disturbance 7.3.1 Outcome statistics versus state updates After any outcome, an outcome-dependent unitary Vj changes the branch operator to VjMj but leaves Ej unchanged. For a sharp Z measurement, compare the ordinary update with an implementation that flips the system after outcome one. The first leaves the observed basis state; the second always leaves zero. Both have exactly the same initial outcome probabilities. The full collection of branch maps is called an instrument. 7.3.2 Sequential measurement and retained records A subsequent measurement can distinguish those instruments. On input one, the ordinary Z instrument followed by another Z measurement returns one twice; the reset-after-readout instrument returns one then zero. Recording, ignoring, and conditioning on an outcome are different operations. A weakly informative measurement can preserve some coherence, but “weak” does not mean nondisturbing for every input or every accumulated sequence. Problem 7.3. Why is a POVM insufficient to predict sequential measurements? Worked solution It specifies each first outcome probability, but not the conditional output state. The next measurement depends on the instrument, including any outcome-dependent transformations. We can now optimize measurements for a concrete information task: guessing which of two known states was prepared."}, {"title": "Discrimination Tasks", "chapter": "State Discrimination", "url": "08-state-discrimination.html#section-8-1", "text": "8.1 Discrimination Tasks Alice chooses zero or plus with equal probability. Bob knows the two possibilities but receives only one qubit. A computational-basis measurement is useful, yet it is not the best possible decision procedure. 8.1.1 Priors, decisions, and error Let the labels be 0 for |0〉 and 1 for |+〉. Measuring Z and guessing 0 after outcome zero, 1 after outcome one succeeds with probability 1/2+1/4=3/4. Always guessing either label succeeds only one half. The prior distribution and whether wrong or inconclusive answers are permitted define the optimization problem. 8.1.2 Orthogonal and nonorthogonal alternatives The overlap is \\(s=|\\braket0+|=1/\\sqrt2\\). Orthogonal states have zero overlap and can be separated by a projective measurement with certainty. These two are nonorthogonal. A perfect distinguishing measurement would have to assign contradictory probabilities to their shared component. The quantitative optimum below shows the unavoidable error rather than merely declaring the states “similar.” Problem 8.1. Compare the Z strategy’s error with the optimal error. Worked solution The Z error is 1/4; the optimum is (1−1/√2)/2≈0.1464. Changing the measurement basis improves performance without changing the encoding."}, {"title": "Optimal Strategies", "chapter": "State Discrimination", "url": "08-state-discrimination.html#section-8-2", "text": "8.2 Optimal Strategies 8.2.1 Minimum-error discrimination For two density operators with priors p0,p1, guessing zero on effect E gives success \\(p_1+\\mathrm{tr}[E(p_0\\rho_0-p_1\\rho_1)]\\). Diagonalize the Hermitian difference Δ. Choosing E as the projector onto its positive-eigenvalue directions maximizes the trace, because each direction can receive a weight between zero and one. The optimum is \\((1+\\|\\Delta\\|_1)/2\\), where the trace norm sums absolute eigenvalues for a Hermitian matrix. For equal-prior zero and plus, Δ=(Z−X)/4 has eigenvalues ±1/(2√2), giving success \\((1+1/\\sqrt2)/2\\approx0.8536\\). The zero-guess direction is \\(\\cos(\\pi/8)\\ket0-\\sin(\\pi/8)\\ket1\\). 8.2.2 Unambiguous discrimination and inconclusive results If mistakes are forbidden but “inconclusive” is allowed, use \\(E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-\\), \\(E_1=c\\ket1\\bra1\\), and \\(E_?=I-E_0-E_1\\), with \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\). E0 never fires on plus and E1 never fires on zero. The chosen c makes the remainder positive. Each input has conclusive probability c/2=1−1/√2; the rest is inconclusive. For two equal-prior pure states this success 1−s is optimal; this optimality is a theorem beyond verifying the explicit effects. Problem 8.2. Why does E0 give no false identification on plus? Worked solution Minus is orthogonal to plus, so 〈+|E0|+〉=0. Positivity ensures this is a valid zero probability, not cancellation between negative probabilities. Minimum-error discrimination for equal-prior zero and plus. After this rotation, guess zero for outcome zero and plus for outcome one."}, {"title": "Accessible Information", "chapter": "State Discrimination", "url": "08-state-discrimination.html#section-8-3", "text": "8.3 Accessible Information 8.3.1 Measurements on encoded messages For an encoding ensemble \\(\\{p_x,\\rho_x\\}\\) and chosen POVM, the classical joint distribution is \\(p(x,y)=p_x\\mathrm{tr}(E_y\\rho_x)\\). Its mutual information is \\(I(X:Y)=\\sum_{x,y}p(x,y)\\log_2[p(x,y)/(p(x)p(y))]\\). This measures how much the outcome reduces uncertainty about the label on average. Maximizing it over measurements defines accessible information. Maximizing decision success and maximizing mutual information need not be the same optimization. 8.3.2 Bounds on classical information extraction A finite-dimensional quantum carrier does not offer unrestricted access to all continuous amplitude parameters in a single shot. Entropy gives a bound on accessible classical information, called the Holevo bound, which we will state after constructing quantum entropy in Chapter 11. Here the operational calculation is already explicit: choose an effect, obtain conditional probabilities, and evaluate the task’s score. Repeated independent copies provide a different resource and can improve discrimination. Problem 8.3. If one state has prior 0.99, is a fair guess the right baseline? Worked solution No. Always guessing the common state already succeeds with probability 0.99. Any claimed improvement must use the specified priors. The distinction between orthogonal and nonorthogonal inputs also limits copying, broadcasting, and signalling. Sources and further study: Watrous, The Theory of Quantum Information, discrimination ."}, {"title": "Copying Restrictions", "chapter": "Information Constraints", "url": "09-information-constraints.html#section-9-1", "text": "9.1 Copying Restrictions Suppose a machine promises to duplicate any unknown pure state. Test it on two nonorthogonal inputs. Preserving their overlap before and after the operation exposes the contradiction. 9.1.1 No-cloning Assume a fixed reversible machine maps \\(\\ket\\psi\\ket0\\ket e\\) to \\(\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\ket{e_\\psi}\\) and similarly for φ. Unitarity requires \\(\\braket\\psi\\phi=(\\braket\\psi\\phi)^2\\braket{e_\\psi}{e_\\phi}\\). Taking magnitudes gives s≤s² because the environment overlap is at most one. For 0 9.1.2 No-broadcasting and mixed states Broadcasting asks only that each output marginal equal the supplied mixed state, allowing correlations between outputs. This is weaker than producing an independent tensor-square copy. A set of density operators can be broadcast by one channel exactly when they commute pairwise, a theorem cited below. Commuting states share a diagonal basis; measuring that classical label and copying it gives both requested marginals. Noncommuting ensembles cannot be treated as merely unknown classical distributions. Problem 9.1. Why does the overlap proof not forbid copying zero and one? Worked solution Their overlap is zero, so the preservation equation reads 0=0. A CNOT with a zero target realizes the copying."}, {"title": "Locality Constraints", "chapter": "Information Constraints", "url": "09-information-constraints.html#section-9-2", "text": "9.2 Locality Constraints 9.2.1 Local operations and remote statistics Let Alice apply a local trace-preserving operation with operators Kj. Bob’s expectation of any observable B afterward is \\(\\sum_j\\mathrm{tr}[(K_j^\\dagger K_j\\otimes B)\\rho_{AB}]=\\mathrm{tr}[(I\\otimes B)\\rho_{AB}]\\). The completeness relation makes it unchanged. Therefore Alice’s unconditioned local operation cannot alter Bob’s local outcome distribution. Shared entanglement does not remove the need for communication. 9.2.2 No-signalling and conditional predictions Conditioning on a particular Alice outcome can change Bob’s predicted state. This does not contradict the previous calculation: conditioning selects a branch rather than summing all branches. Bob needs the classical record to identify which of his trials belongs to that branch. Sorting data afterward can reveal strong correlations without transmitting a message before that record arrives. Problem 9.2. Does deleting Alice’s measurement record restore Bob’s knowledge of her outcome? Worked solution No. It removes the conditioning information. Bob retains the unconditioned reduced state and cannot infer which branch occurred from an arbitrary single local measurement."}, {"title": "Scope of Impossibility", "chapter": "Information Constraints", "url": "09-information-constraints.html#section-9-3", "text": "9.3 Scope of Impossibility 9.3.1 Restricted input sets The cloning argument allows orthogonal inputs because s=0 is consistent. CNOT copies computational-basis states into a zero target, but on plus it creates a Bell state instead of two plus states. A known fixed state can be prepared repeatedly from its description. The impossibility concerns one device acting exactly and deterministically on an unknown member of an unrestricted nonorthogonal set. 9.3.2 Approximate and probabilistic alternatives Approximate cloning can trade fidelity against disturbance, and probabilistic exact cloning is possible for suitable restricted linearly independent sets with a declared failure outcome. Neither contradicts deterministic no-cloning. A security or impossibility argument must specify which resources, input sets, approximation, and conditioning are allowed. Proving a restricted impossibility is useful only if the actual protocol satisfies those restrictions. Problem 9.3. Would a copier for a known single state violate the theorem? Worked solution No. There are no conflicting nonorthogonal alternatives to preserve. A known preparation circuit can produce as many copies as its resources permit. Entanglement protocols respect these constraints by explicitly consuming shared pairs and sending classical or quantum systems. Sources and further study: Barnum et al., Noncommuting mixed states cannot be broadcast . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "Teleportation", "chapter": "Entanglement Protocols", "url": "10-entanglement-protocols.html#section-10-1", "text": "10.1 Teleportation Alice has an unknown qubit and shares a Bell pair with Bob. She cannot copy the input or describe its amplitudes from one sample. Nevertheless, a joint measurement and two classical bits can transfer its state to Bob. 10.1.1 Joint measurements and outcome branches Use register order S,A,B. Start with \\(\\ket\\psi_S\\ket{\\Phi^+}_{AB}\\). Alice applies CNOT S→A, then H on S. Expansion gives \\[\\frac12\\sum_{m,n=0}^1\\ket{mn}_{SA}X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi_B.\\] Each pair of outcomes m,n has probability one quarter, independent of the unknown input. This equation is the complete branch table: 00 gives ψ, 01 gives Xψ, 10 gives Zψ, and 11 gives XZψ. 10.1.2 Conditional corrections and resource consumption Alice sends m,n. Bob applies X when n=1 and then Z when m=1, implementing \\(Z^mX^n\\) and recovering ψ. Alice’s input has been measured and the shared Bell pair consumed. Before the record arrives, averaging the four Pauli branches gives I/2 on Bob. The protocol transmits one unknown qubit using one shared ebit and two classical bits; it does not send a usable message instantly. Problem 10.1. For teleportation outcome m=1,n=0, what correction is required? Worked solution Bob holds Zψ and applies Z. Applying X instead generally gives the wrong state. Teleportation with classical feedforward. Alice’s top measurement gives m and the middle gives n; Bob applies X^n then Z^m using the communicated records. No quantum control from the measured wires is implied."}, {"title": "Dense Coding", "chapter": "Entanglement Protocols", "url": "10-entanglement-protocols.html#section-10-2", "text": "10.2 Dense Coding 10.2.1 Encoding classical messages in a shared pair For dense coding, begin with a Bell pair and let Alice apply X^bZ^a to her half to encode two classical bits a,b. The four resulting Bell states are orthogonal. Alice sends her physical qubit to Bob; he now holds both halves and can perform a joint measurement. Pre-shared entanglement alone would not let him read the choice. 10.2.2 Joint decoding and transmitted systems Decoding uses CNOT from Alice’s received qubit to Bob’s original half, followed by H on the received qubit and computational-basis measurement. The outputs are a,b under this convention, up to irrelevant global branch phases in the intermediate Bell states. The resource account is one transmitted qubit plus one consumed shared Bell pair for two classical bits. Comparing with an unassisted qubit while ignoring the prior pair would use unequal resources. Problem 10.2. Why does dense coding not contradict a one-bit unassisted qubit bound? Worked solution It consumes pre-shared entanglement and uses a joint two-qubit decoder. The resource model differs from sending an unassisted qubit."}, {"title": "Entanglement Swapping", "chapter": "Entanglement Protocols", "url": "10-entanglement-protocols.html#section-10-3", "text": "10.3 Entanglement Swapping 10.3.1 Connecting separated pairs If A–B and C–D are Bell pairs, a Bell measurement on B,C leaves A,D in a Bell state conditioned on its outcome. This is entanglement swapping. Expand the two pairs and group terms by the Bell basis of the middle systems to obtain four equally likely branches. The endpoints need not interact directly, but creating the initial pairs and sending the measurement record still require physical resources. 10.3.2 Classical records and protocol accounting Without the swapping outcome, the endpoints average to I/4 and cannot select a particular Bell state. Communicating the record lets them correct or track a Pauli frame. In a chain, loss, imperfect entanglement, memories, and classical messages limit the practical rate. Ideal swapping is a protocol component, not by itself a complete scalable repeater architecture. Problem 10.3. Can the endpoints use a particular swapped Bell state before learning the middle result? Worked solution They cannot identify its Pauli frame. Their unconditioned joint state is the equal mixture of four Bell states, I/4. We next quantify uncertainty and correlations, including the striking fact that a pure pair can have uncertain individual parts. Sources and further study: Bennett et al., Teleporting an unknown quantum state ; Bennett and Wiesner, Dense coding ."}, {"title": "State Entropy", "chapter": "Quantum Entropy", "url": "11-quantum-entropy.html#section-11-1", "text": "11.1 State Entropy A Bell pair is specified by one pure vector, yet either half is maximally uncertain. A useful entropy must describe both facts without treating their difference as an inconsistency. 11.1.1 Eigenvalues as probabilities A density matrix has orthogonal eigenvectors satisfying \\(\\rho\\ket j=\\lambda_j\\ket j\\). Positivity makes the eigenvalues nonnegative and trace one makes them sum to one. In that basis, \\(\\rho=\\sum_j\\lambda_j\\ket j\\bra j\\). For \\(\\rho=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&r\\\\r&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) with 0≤r≤1, plus and minus have eigenvalues (1±r)/2. These values characterize the state even when its preparation ensemble uses different nonorthogonal vectors. 11.1.2 Von Neumann entropy and pure states Define \\(S(\\rho)=-\\sum_j\\lambda_j\\log_2\\lambda_j\\), equivalently −tr(ρ log₂ρ), where the matrix logarithm applies the scalar logarithm in the eigenbasis. Zero eigenvalues contribute zero by continuity. A pure state has eigenvalues 1,0,… and entropy zero; I/2 has entropy one. This is not the entropy of every measurement: measuring a pure plus state in Z gives a fair classical record despite S=0. Problem 11.1. What is the entropy of diag(3/4,1/4)? Worked solution It is −(3/4)log₂(3/4)−(1/4)log₂(1/4)≈0.811 bits."}, {"title": "Correlation Measures", "chapter": "Quantum Entropy", "url": "11-quantum-entropy.html#section-11-2", "text": "11.2 Correlation Measures 11.2.1 Mutual information The quantum mutual information is \\(I(A:B)=S(A)+S(B)-S(AB)\\). For a Bell pair it is 1+1−0=2 bits. For the equal classical mixture of 00 and 11 it is 1+1−1=1. The extra quantum correlation is not a claim that measuring either half alone yields two classical bits. Measurements and assistance determine how correlations become usable. 11.2.2 Conditional entropy and negative values Conditional entropy is \\(S(A|B)=S(AB)-S(B)\\). It equals −1 for a Bell pair and zero for the classically correlated mixture. Negative conditional entropy is meaningful in resource tasks: in asymptotic quantum state merging, with classical communication available, the conditional entropy gives the net entanglement cost and a negative value represents entanglement generation. This operational theorem requires many independent copies and vanishing error; it is not an ordinary negative count of classical guesses. Problem 11.2. Why is the Bell pair’s joint entropy zero? Worked solution Its joint density matrix is a rank-one projector with one eigenvalue one. The mixed local spectra do not determine the joint spectrum."}, {"title": "Information Inequalities", "chapter": "Quantum Entropy", "url": "11-quantum-entropy.html#section-11-3", "text": "11.3 Information Inequalities 11.3.1 Relative entropy and distinguishability Relative entropy is \\(D(\\rho\\|\\sigma)=\\mathrm{tr}[\\rho(\\log_2\\rho-\\log_2\\sigma)]\\), finite when the support of ρ lies inside that of σ. It quantifies asymmetry in distinguishing state hypotheses and is not a symmetric geometric distance. For ρ=|0〉〈0| and σ=I/2 it is one; reversing them gives infinity because σ then assigns zero support to a possible input component. 11.3.2 Data processing and operational meaning Quantum relative entropy cannot increase under a common channel: \\(D(\\mathcal E(\\rho)\\|\\mathcal E(\\sigma))\\leq D(\\rho\\|\\sigma)\\). This is a theorem, not proved by the examples here. Applying information inequalities to a classical label encoded as states gives the Holevo bound \\(I(X:Y)\\leq\\chi=S(\\sum_xp_x\\rho_x)-\\sum_xp_xS(\\rho_x)\\). For equal-prior pure zero and plus, the average eigenvalues are (1±1/√2)/2, so χ≈0.601 bits. It bounds every measurement’s accessible information but need not be achieved by a single-copy measurement. This is distinct from the 0.8536 decision success in Chapter 8. Problem 11.3. Does χ=0.601 mean each trial returns a fractional bit string? Worked solution No. It bounds average mutual information between the input label and the recorded outcome. Individual outcomes are ordinary labels; information is a statistical quantity. Entropy next becomes a physical resource rate: the number of qubits needed to preserve a repeated quantum source. Sources and further study: Watrous, The Theory of Quantum Information, entropy ; Horodecki, Oppenheim and Winter, Quantum state merging ."}, {"title": "Quantum Sources", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "12-quantum-compression.html#section-12-1", "text": "12.1 Quantum Sources A source emits pure quantum signals independently, but its average state has eigenvalues 3/4 and 1/4. Sending one qubit per signal works. Can a long block be stored in fewer qubits while preserving the signals, including their coherence with other systems? 12.1.1 Source ensembles and average states An ensemble \\(\\{p_x,\\ket{\\psi_x}\\}\\) has average state \\(\\rho=\\sum_xp_x\\ket{\\psi_x}\\bra{\\psi_x}\\). For independent identical uses, the average block state is \\(\\rho^{\\otimes n}\\). The eigenbasis of ρ need not coincide with the source’s prepared states. Compression should preserve the quantum signal, not merely the classical label of an eigenbasis measurement. Measuring each signal first can destroy the information the task asks us to preserve. 12.1.2 Faithful recovery and fidelity For a pure target ψ and recovered state σ, the squared-fidelity convention is \\(F=\\bra\\psi\\sigma\\ket\\psi\\), equal to one for exact recovery. For general states use \\(F(\\rho,\\sigma)=(\\mathrm{tr}\\sqrt{\\sqrt\\rho\\sigma\\sqrt\\rho})^2\\). We specify the square because another common convention takes its square root. Preserving entanglement with a reference is a strong recovery criterion: the encoder should not secretly measure and reconstruct an incompatible approximation. Problem 12.1. For n=3 and eigenvalue 1/4 for label one, what total probability lies on strings with zero or one ones? Worked solution It is \\((3/4)^3+3(1/4)(3/4)^2=54/64=0.84375\\). These four strings span a space that can be labeled with two qubits, but the discarded weight is still substantial. This is a finite illustration, not a high-fidelity asymptotic guarantee."}, {"title": "Typical Subspaces", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "12-quantum-compression.html#section-12-2", "text": "12.2 Typical Subspaces 12.2.1 Repeated sources and typicality In the eigenbasis of ρ, each n-letter string has probability \\(\\lambda_{j_1}\\cdots\\lambda_{j_n}\\). Its information per letter is the average of −log₂λj. For independent repetitions, most weight lies on strings whose information per letter is within δ of S(ρ). Their span is the typical subspace. Each typical string has probability at least \\(2^{-n(S+\\delta)}\\), so there are at most \\(2^{n(S+\\delta)}\\) of them. Their total probability tends to one as n grows. 12.2.2 Encoding and decoding Assign orthogonal compressed labels to the typical eigenstrings and extend this assignment linearly. The decoder reverses that embedding. A projection distinguishing typical from atypical space can be part of the construction, but individual typical strings must not be measured: their superpositions need to remain coherent. For atypical outcomes use a failure procedure. The high retained weight supplies the fidelity guarantee as the block length grows. Problem 12.2. Why not measure the typical string and send its index classically? Worked solution That would preserve the eigenbasis label but destroy superpositions of different strings and their coherence with a reference. Quantum compression preserves the state in the subspace."}, {"title": "Compression Limits", "chapter": "Quantum Compression", "url": "12-quantum-compression.html#section-12-3", "text": "12.3 Compression Limits 12.3.1 Schumacher compression Schumacher compression states that, for a finite-dimensional memoryless quantum source under the standard asymptotically faithful quantum recovery criterion, the entropy S(ρ) is the compression rate in qubits per signal. Rates above it are achievable and rates below it cannot maintain asymptotically faithful recovery. The typical-space argument explains achievability’s geometry; the full converse and fidelity analysis are theorem-level results cited below. For eigenvalues 3/4,1/4 the limiting rate is about 0.811 qubits per signal. 12.3.2 Rates, errors, and assumptions At finite n, integer dimensions, atypical probability, and circuit costs matter. Entropy does not promise that a three-signal block fits faithfully into 2.433 physical qubits. If the source has memory, is unknown, or must preserve every possible input exactly rather than typical source behavior, the stated theorem does not apply unchanged. A compressible source also does not imply that an arbitrary computation can discard most of its qubits safely. Problem 12.3. What compression rate follows for the maximally mixed qubit source? Worked solution Its entropy is one, so there is no asymptotic reduction below one qubit per signal under faithful quantum recovery. Typical subspaces also explain how many weakly entangled pairs can supply a smaller number of nearly perfect Bell pairs. Sources and further study: Schumacher, Quantum coding ."}, {"title": "Allowed Operations", "chapter": "Entanglement Transformations", "url": "13-entanglement-transformations.html#section-13-1", "text": "13.1 Allowed Operations Alice and Bob share √p|00〉+√(1−p)|11〉, with p at least one half. Can local actions turn this into a Bell pair? The answer changes depending on whether success must be certain, failures may be declared, or many copies are available. 13.1.1 Local operations and classical communication Local operations and classical communication, abbreviated LOCC, allow each party to act on their own systems and exchange classical outcomes. They do not include sending a quantum system or applying a joint gate across the separation. Local unitaries can change basis but cannot alter the amount of pure-state entanglement. Measurements can change it in selected branches, so branch probabilities must be included. 13.1.2 Schmidt coefficients and conversion criteria A pure joint state can be written \\(\\sum_j\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}\\ket{u_j}\\ket{v_j}\\) in orthonormal local bases. This Schmidt form follows by diagonalizing a reduced density matrix and choosing corresponding partner vectors; the λj are its eigenvalues. Nielsen’s deterministic pure-state conversion criterion is \\(\\lambda(\\psi)\\prec\\lambda(\\phi)\\) for conversion ψ→φ: each partial sum of the largest input coefficients must be no larger than the corresponding output sum. The sums agree at one. Thus (1/2,1/2) can become (p,1−p), but the reverse is impossible deterministically for p>1/2. Problem 13.1. At p=0.9, what is the filter’s success probability? Worked solution It is 2(0.1)=0.2. The successful state is maximally entangled, but four fifths of trials fail, so selecting successes does not create free entanglement."}, {"title": "Pure-State Resources", "chapter": "Entanglement Transformations", "url": "13-entanglement-transformations.html#section-13-2", "text": "13.2 Pure-State Resources 13.2.1 Concentration A probabilistic filter on Alice gives a concrete alternative. Set \\(K=\\mathrm{diag}(\\sqrt{(1-p)/p},1)\\). On success it changes the joint state to \\(\\sqrt{1-p}(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})\\), a Bell pair after normalization, with probability 2(1−p). The complementary failure operator \\(L=\\mathrm{diag}(\\sqrt{(2p-1)/p},0)\\) satisfies K†K+L†L=I. At p=3/4, half the trials succeed and half leave a product state. The successful branch alone would overstate the resource gain. 13.2.2 Dilution and asymptotic rates For many independent copies of a bipartite pure state, concentration produces Bell pairs at asymptotic rate h₂(p); dilution uses Bell pairs at the same asymptotic rate to prepare the state, with errors tending to zero and classical communication available. The typical Schmidt strings carry this rate, closely related to compression. This asymptotic reversibility differs from the single-copy deterministic restriction. It does not provide an exact finite-block conversion with fractional Bell pairs. Problem 13.2. Can a product state with Schmidt vector (1,0) become a Bell pair deterministically by LOCC? Worked solution No. The first majorization inequality would require 1≤1/2. The filter also gives zero success as p approaches one."}, {"title": "Mixed-State Resources", "chapter": "Entanglement Transformations", "url": "13-entanglement-transformations.html#section-13-3", "text": "13.3 Mixed-State Resources 13.3.1 Distillation and formation A mixed state is separable if it is a convex mixture of product states; otherwise it is entangled. Distillable entanglement asks for the best Bell-pair rate obtainable from many copies by a specified LOCC protocol class. Entanglement cost asks how many Bell pairs are needed to create those copies. An inaccessible environmental record can make reversing a mixed-state preparation much harder than reversing a pure-state conversion. 13.3.2 Irreversibility and limitations For mixed states, creation and distillation rates need not agree. Bound entanglement gives examples that are entangled but cannot supply Bell pairs by the allowed distillation operations. These are theorem-level existence statements, not consequences of the two-coefficient filter. Always specify whether conversion is deterministic, stochastic, approximate, asymptotic, and one-way or two-way assisted. “More entangled” is not a complete ordering of all state-conversion tasks. Problem 13.3. Why does an asymptotic rate not determine a one-copy conversion probability? Worked solution The rate describes collective protocols on many copies with vanishing approximation error. A finite-copy protocol has different success and exactness constraints. Preserving entanglement under noise leads to quantum error correction, where the environment must not learn the logical state. Sources and further study: Nielsen, Conditions for entanglement transformations ; Bennett et al., Concentrating partial entanglement ; Horodecki et al., Mixed-state entanglement and irreversibility ."}, {"title": "Encoding", "chapter": "Quantum Error Correction", "url": "14-quantum-error-correction.html#section-14-1", "text": "14.1 Encoding An unknown qubit cannot be copied into three independent specimens. Yet it can be encoded into a joint three-qubit state whose error can be diagnosed without learning its amplitudes. 14.1.1 Redundancy without cloning With two zero auxiliaries, apply CNOTs from the input to each auxiliary. The result is \\(\\alpha\\ket{000}+\\beta\\ket{111}\\), not \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)^{\\otimes3}\\). The two-dimensional span of 000 and 111 is the code space. It stores one logical qubit across three physical qubits. The encoding respects no-cloning because the information is in the joint state. 14.1.2 Errors on joint states A bit flip on qubit one, with labels 1,2,3 from left to right, produces \\(\\alpha\\ket{100}+\\beta\\ket{011}\\). Neither amplitude is revealed by the fact that one bit disagrees with its neighbors. Single X errors move the code into distinct two-dimensional subspaces. A phase flip Z instead changes the relative sign within the code, an error this repetition code will not detect. Problem 14.1. An observed syndrome is (−,−). Which correction is used? Worked solution Apply X to the middle data qubit. It anticommutes with both parity checks, producing two negative signs. Three-qubit repetition encoding produces α|000⟩+β|111⟩. This is joint encoding, not three independent copies."}, {"title": "Detection and Recovery", "chapter": "Quantum Error Correction", "url": "14-quantum-error-correction.html#section-14-2", "text": "14.2 Detection and Recovery 14.2.1 Syndromes without logical readout Measure the commuting parity observables Z1Z2 and Z2Z3. The no-error syndrome is (+,+); X1 gives (−,+), X2 gives (−,−), and X3 gives (+,−). Each parity can be measured by computing the XOR of its two data bits into a zero auxiliary with two CNOTs and measuring only that auxiliary. Both logical codewords have the same parity, so the result reveals the error class without distinguishing α from β. 14.2.2 Conditional recovery Apply the corresponding X correction to the indicated qubit. Then decode with the inverse of the two encoding CNOTs if the logical state is needed on one wire. For a coherent combination of correctable errors, syndrome measurement separates branches, and each recovery returns the same logical state. The measurement is allowed to learn the error, but not the encoded amplitudes. Faulty syndrome circuits require additional design; this ideal recovery is not yet fault tolerance. Problem 14.2. Why not measure all three data bits and take a majority? Worked solution That would distinguish 000 from 111 and collapse the logical superposition. Parity measurements reveal only the relative disagreement, leaving α and β coherent. A Z-parity measurement. The auxiliary records the XOR of two data bits, revealing agreement without reading either logical basis value separately."}, {"title": "Correction Conditions", "chapter": "Quantum Error Correction", "url": "14-quantum-error-correction.html#section-14-3", "text": "14.3 Correction Conditions 14.3.1 Correctable error sets For a code projector P and error operators Ea, exact correction is possible precisely when \\(P E_a^\\dagger E_b P=c_{ab}P\\). This Knill–Laflamme criterion says the overlaps of error images do not reveal which logical state was present. For the repetition code and {I,X1,X2,X3}, different error images are orthogonal and equal-error overlaps are P, so the criterion holds. It also covers linear combinations of those operators. Correcting arbitrary single-qubit noise would require handling X,Y,Z on each qubit, a larger error set. 14.3.2 Code capabilities and limitations Two bit flips can mimic the syndrome of the remaining single flip. Majority recovery then makes a logical error. Under independent bit-flip probability p, the failure probability is 3p²−2p³, matching the classical repetition expression, but the recovered object is a coherent logical qubit. A single Z1 acts as logical Z without changing either parity, so this code offers no general phase protection. Always name the corrected error set and the assumptions on the recovery hardware. Problem 14.3. Does Z1 satisfy the correction criterion together with identity? Worked solution No. PZ1P acts differently on the two logical basis states and is not a scalar multiple of P. The environment could distinguish a logical phase effect. Codes protect states across repeated noisy uses. Channel capacity asks the best rate achievable by all such coding strategies. Sources and further study: Knill and Laflamme, A Theory of Quantum Error-Correcting Codes ."}, {"title": "Communication Tasks", "chapter": "Quantum Communication", "url": "15-quantum-communication.html#section-15-1", "text": "15.1 Communication Tasks A channel sometimes transmits a qubit perfectly and sometimes replaces it with an orthogonal erasure flag. The receiver knows which uses were erased. This simple channel separates classical transmission, quantum transmission, and assisted transmission. 15.1.1 Classical and quantum messages Classical transmission asks the receiver to identify a message label. Quantum transmission asks an unknown state, potentially entangled with a reference, to survive the encoding, channel, and decoding. A device that measures and reprepares basis labels may be a good classical channel while destroying quantum coherence. The reliability criterion must match the requested kind of information. 15.1.2 Assistance and resource models Specify available assistance: pre-shared entanglement, one-way or two-way classical communication, and any other side channels. Teleportation shows that an ebit plus two classical bits can transmit a qubit, while dense coding changes classical capacity with entanglement assistance. These are different resource models, so their rates cannot be compared without their resource accounts. Problem 15.1. Why does a known erasure differ from an unflagged bit flip? Worked solution The receiver knows the failed positions and can adapt decoding to that side information. The channel output alphabet includes a flag distinct from the qubit subspace."}, {"title": "Coding and Rates", "chapter": "Quantum Communication", "url": "15-quantum-communication.html#section-15-2", "text": "15.2 Coding and Rates 15.2.1 Achievability and converse statements A rate R is achievable if a sequence of n-use codes transmits about nR bits or qubits with error tending to zero. A converse excludes rates above the capacity under the same criterion. A finite demonstration with a small error does not establish either asymptotic claim. We assume finite-dimensional memoryless channels, meaning independent uses of the same channel map, unless stated otherwise. 15.2.2 Classical and entanglement-assisted capacities For unassisted classical communication, define \\(\\chi^*(\\mathcal N)\\) as the maximum output Holevo information over input ensembles. General classical capacity is the regularized quantity \\(C(\\mathcal N)=\\sup_n\\chi^*(\\mathcal N^{\\otimes n})/n\\), allowing entangled encodings and collective decoding. With unlimited prior entanglement, the classical capacity is the single-use maximum of \\(I(R:B)\\) after sending one half of a purification through the channel. These coding theorems are stated here, not derived by the examples; single-use simplification without assistance requires additional structure. Problem 15.2. What happens at e=1/2? Worked solution Classical capacity is 1/2 bit per use, entanglement-assisted classical capacity is one bit per use, and unassisted quantum capacity is zero. The tasks and resources are different."}, {"title": "Quantum Transmission", "chapter": "Quantum Communication", "url": "15-quantum-communication.html#section-15-3", "text": "15.3 Quantum Transmission 15.3.1 Coherent information and quantum capacity For input ρ purified by RA and output state ωRB, coherent information is \\(I_c(\\rho,\\mathcal N)=S(B)_\\omega-S(RB)_\\omega\\). Unassisted quantum capacity is \\(Q(\\mathcal N)=\\sup_n n^{-1}\\max_{\\rho_n} I_c(\\rho_n,\\mathcal N^{\\otimes n})\\). The optimization includes correlations across inputs; one use need not attain the capacity. This is an asymptotic coding theorem. A large positive value for a particular input is useful evidence of an achievable rate, but a general converse needs the full theorem. 15.3.2 Regularization and finite-resource limitations For the qubit erasure channel with erasure probability e, the exact capacities are C=1−e classical bits, entanglement-assisted classical capacity 2(1−e) bits, and unassisted Q=max(0,1−2e) qubits per use. These are established channel-specific theorems. With a maximally entangled reference, \\(S(B)=h_2(e)+(1-e)\\) and \\(S(RB)=h_2(e)+e\\), so coherent information is 1−2e. At e=1/4, these three capacities are 3/4,3/2,1/2. Two-way classical assistance changes the quantum rate; do not silently use it in the unassisted claim. Finite block length, decoding effort, and experimental noise remain practical constraints. Problem 15.3. Why is a single-use coherent information not generally “the quantum capacity”? Worked solution Block encodings can use correlations across channel inputs and improve the per-use value. The regularized maximization is essential without a theorem proving additivity for the channel. Communication rates describe reliability. The final chapter asks when transmitted data can also be secret from an adversary. Sources and further study: Holevo, Capacity with general signal states ; Bennett et al., Entanglement-assisted capacity ; Devetak, Private classical and quantum capacity ; Bennett et al., Erasure channel capacities ."}, {"title": "Key Distribution", "chapter": "Quantum Cryptography", "url": "16-quantum-cryptography.html#section-16-1", "text": "16.1 Key Distribution Alice and Bob want a shared random key that an eavesdropper cannot predict. Quantum transmission can help them estimate how much information may have leaked, but detection of disturbance alone is not a security proof. 16.1.1 Preparation and basis choices In ideal BB84, Alice chooses a random bit and independently chooses the Z or X basis, sending zero/one or plus/minus accordingly. Bob independently chooses one of those bases. They later announce bases over an authenticated public channel and retain only matching-basis rounds. The transmitted bits remain secret; the basis announcements need not be secret. About half of the ideal rounds survive this sifting when bases are equally likely. 16.1.2 Entanglement-based descriptions An equivalent ideal description lets Alice prepare a Bell pair, send one half, and measure her own half in a chosen basis. Her outcome prepares the corresponding state remotely, conditioned on the record. This connects key distribution to entanglement verification and error correction. It does not remove source or detector assumptions: an actual prepare-and-measure implementation must meet the conditions used in its proof. Problem 16.1. Why is the intercept-resend error 1/4 rather than 1/2? Worked solution Half of Eve’s basis choices match and cause no bit error on ideal sifted rounds. In the other half, Bob is wrong half the time, giving (1/2)(1/2)=1/4. An ideal BB84 signal: Alice selects bit x and basis a; Bob selects basis b. The wire between preparation and readout is the transmitted quantum system. Keep rounds with a=b."}, {"title": "From Data to a Key", "chapter": "Quantum Cryptography", "url": "16-quantum-cryptography.html#section-16-2", "text": "16.2 From Data to a Key 16.2.1 Parameter estimation and error correction They publicly reveal a randomly chosen subset to estimate error rates and discard those test bits from the key. Remaining discrepancies are reconciled by classical communication; that public transcript leaks information and must be included in the security account. Finite samples only bound unobserved errors with a stated failure probability. Zero observed errors in a small sample is not proof of a noiseless or attack-free channel. 16.2.2 Privacy amplification Privacy amplification compresses the reconciled string with a publicly chosen hash so that the shorter result is nearly uniform and independent of the adversary’s information. The output length depends on a proved entropy bound after accounting for leakage and finite-data penalties. A public hash seed need not be secret, but it must satisfy the assumptions of the chosen extractor or hashing theorem. Choosing an arbitrary short checksum is not a privacy-amplification proof. Problem 16.2. Can Alice and Bob keep the publicly revealed test bits as secret key? Worked solution No. Those bit values are public. They estimate channel statistics and must be excluded from the secret output."}, {"title": "Security Assumptions", "chapter": "Quantum Cryptography", "url": "16-quantum-cryptography.html#section-16-3", "text": "16.3 Security Assumptions 16.3.1 Eavesdropping models and authentication Consider the intercept-resend attack: Eve chooses Z or X at random, measures, and resends her result. On sifted rounds she chose the wrong basis with probability one half; in those rounds Bob’s bit is wrong with probability one half. The resulting error rate is 1/4. This calculation explains one observable disturbance, but coherent attacks can interact jointly with signals and preserve quantum memory. Security must be proved against the declared adversary class. An authenticated classical channel is essential: otherwise an attacker can impersonate both parties. 16.3.2 Finite data and composable security goals A composable secrecy goal compares the joint key-adversary state with an ideal uniform independent key: \\(\\frac12\\|\\rho_{KE}-\\tau_K\\otimes\\rho_E\\|_1\\leq\\epsilon_{\\rm sec}\\), with abort probability handled in the protocol’s formal definition. Correctness separately bounds the probability that Alice and Bob disagree. In an ideal asymptotic single-photon BB84 model with suitable symmetry and one-way processing, the familiar rate expression is \\(1-2h_2(Q)\\) per sifted bit when positive; it is not a universal finite-device key formula. Actual security accounts for preparation, detectors, loss, authentication, error correction, finite statistics, and information leaked during processing. The cited proof explains the ideal reduction; a protocol demonstration alone does not certify a secure implementation. Problem 16.3. Why is secrecy alone insufficient if Alice’s and Bob’s keys differ? Worked solution A key must also be correct for its intended use. Composable definitions separately control disagreement and deviation from uniform independence, with a stated total failure budget. The course has moved from distinguishable messages to the operational limits of learning, preserving, transmitting, and protecting quantum information. Each rate or security claim depends on the same discipline: define the task, permitted resources, assumptions, and error criterion before asserting what is possible. Sources and further study: Shor and Preskill, Security of BB84 ; Renner, Security of Quantum Key Distribution ."}];
